---
// Help: correcting a record's links (docs/CONTRIBUTIONS.md, "Correct a record"; the Contribute
// section, src/components/paper/Contribute.astro; oscr/jobs.py, link_edit).
import Info from "../../components/Info.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";

const toc = [
  { id: "who", text: "Who may correct" },
  { id: "what", text: "What can be corrected" },
  { id: "how", text: "How" },
  { id: "after", text: "What happens next" },
  { id: "else", text: "Anything else wrong" },
];
---
<Info
  title="Correcting a record's links"
  description={`How a paper's authors and its code's maintainers correct its code and data links on ${SITE_NAME}, and how each correction becomes a new version.`}
  group="help"
  path="/help/correct/"
  parents={[{ text: "Help", href: "/help/" }]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead" slot="lead">
    The harvester can miss a repository, take data for code, or cite a tool the authors only used. The people who know
    best — the paper's authors and its code's maintainers — can correct the record's links themselves.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who">Who may correct</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A <strong>verified author</strong> of the paper (<a href="/help/accounts/#roles">how one becomes one</a>): any of
      its links.</li>
    <li>A <strong>maintainer</strong> of one of its code repositories — its owner, or a public member of its
      organization, on GitHub (or a maintainer the operator made): that repository — say what it is, or remove it — and
      new links.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A contributor of a repository is not enough, though GitHub shows one as a maintainer: one merged pull request makes
    one. A contributor may ask the paper's authors, who can correct the record
    (<a href="/policies/moderation/#verified-only">what only verified people can do</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="what">What can be corrected</h2>
  <p>Links only, in well-defined changes — never free text or markup:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>add</strong> a link, as the authors' code or as their data (checked like a submission's: a place the
      registry knows, and answering);</li>
    <li><strong>remove</strong> a link;</li>
    <li><strong>say what a link is</strong>: the authors' code, their data, or a tool they used.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The bibliographic record — title, authors, journal — comes from the paper's own published metadata and is not
    edited here: a mistake in it is the publisher's to correct.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how">How</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>Open the paper's page, signed in, and go to its <strong>Contribute</strong> section.</li>
    <li>Under "Correct its record", choose the changes (up to ten at once) and, if you wish, a short note (500 characters, no address).</li>
    <li>Send. The site checks each added link at once and records the correction.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="after">What happens next</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE_NAME}'s harvester applies the correction: it verifies the added repositories (commit, licence, scripts),
    concludes the paper's status again, and stores a <strong>new version</strong> of the record. A correction outlives
    every later scan of the paper: the machine's own reading never overrules a person's. The page's Versions section
    lists it as "a correction by a verified author" or "by a maintainer of its code" — never by name; who made it stays
    on the operator's computer. Your account page shows its status (queued, applied or refused, with the reason).
  </p>

  <h2 id="else">Anything else wrong</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A match that pairs the wrong paragraph and lines: say it in a correction's note.</li>
    <li>You are an author but not recognized: <a href="/help/claim/">claim the paper</a>.</li>
    <li>Something should not be on the site at all: <a href="/help/removal/">request its removal</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>At most 20 corrections a day per account.</p>
</Info>
